$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>How the matter reached the Court</h2>
<p>The contractor&rsquo;s Section 9 petition (O.M.P.(I)(COMM) 538/2025) became the vehicle for reference: on 24 December 2025 the disputes went to arbitration, the petition was directed to be treated as an application under Section 17 before the tribunal, and the Ministry of Tourism was impleaded. The sole arbitrator&rsquo;s order of 16 April 2026 then did three things Wapcos found objectionable &mdash; removed the Ministry from the array, directed foreclosure with a certificate to that effect, and permitted the contractor to demobilise its plant and machinery.</p>

<h2>The appellant&rsquo;s two attacks</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Only we may foreclose.</strong> Clause 13 of the General Conditions of Contract vests the power of foreclosure in Wapcos; the tribunal could not hand the contractor an exit the contract reserved to the employer&rsquo;s side.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The Ministry had to stay.</strong> As the project owner funding the work, the Ministry of Tourism was a necessary party, and its deletion left the reference incomplete.</div>
</div>

<h2>Why both failed</h2>
<p>On Clause 13, the Court accepted that the clause &ldquo;does empower the appellant to foreclose the contract/project&rdquo; &mdash; but found nothing in it that <em>proscribes</em> the contractor from seeking foreclosure. Reading the contract with Sections 51 and 53 to 55 of the Indian Contract Act, 1872 (reciprocal promises and the consequences of preventing performance), the contractor who had completed about 90% of the work and then faced payment default was not disentitled from requiring the contract to be foreclosed. The Court further noted that the Clause 13 point appeared to have been raised for the first time in appeal, never before the tribunal, and that the direction to issue a foreclosure certificate was merely consequential.</p>
<p>On the Ministry&rsquo;s deletion, the Court observed that the Ministry was undeniably not a signatory to the contract between Wapcos and the contractor, and that whether Clause 2(6) could bind a non-signatory was prima facie doubtful. The tribunal&rsquo;s reasoning was neither perverse nor patently illegal, and Wapcos had not even contended that the tribunal exceeded its jurisdiction. The appeal was dismissed without costs.</p>

<h2>The working rule for interim-order appeals</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Raise it before the tribunal first.</strong> A contractual clause not urged before the arbitrator is a weak foundation for a Section 37(2)(b) appeal.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Perversity, not preference.</strong> The appellate court will not substitute its reading of the contract for a plausible tribunal view &mdash; the test mirrors award-stage review.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Interim means interim.</strong> Findings at the Section 17 stage are prima facie; the merits of accounts, delay and default remain open in the main reference.</div>
</div>
<p>For parties to government-linked construction contracts, the judgment is a reminder that suspension of work over payment defaults, followed by a Section 17 application, can produce a practically irreversible foreclosure long before any award &mdash; and that the appellate window against such orders is deliberately narrow.</p>
<div class="note">This article reports a judgment of the High Court of Delhi for general information only. It is not legal advice and does not comment on any pending proceeding.</div>
